  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.3 × loss. It is steepest along x (left-right), so steps of learning rate above 0.5 overshoot and grow there. The run starts at x = 1, z = 1.7.",
    "Mini-batch step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: it is largest at 1 and exactly 0 at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 2× their true length, one scale for both. The purple arrow is the step the ball takes next. The dark arrow is −learning rate × the true slope. The step length readout is the purple step's true length.",
    "Diverged means the ball left the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| > 2) or the loss went above 10. The ball and path are then held at the rim, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished). While running, it takes 5 steps a second at 1× speed. Noise comes from a fixed sequence, so a new run uses the next values and Reset replays from the start.",
    "With noise, a single step can raise the loss; the loss falls only on average."
  ]
}